Bound the DRM_VMW_EXECBUF retry loop and stop abort()ing on -EBUSY.

vmw_ioctl_command() retried DRM_VMW_EXECBUF forever while the kernel returned
-EBUSY (vmw_resource_validate(): "Out of device device resources", i.e. device
resources exhausted with nothing evictable) or -ERESTART.  There was no
timeout, no retry cap and no exit path, so a submitting process could spin
indefinitely; when that process is a compositor -- SurfaceFlinger on ARC, in
the case that prompted this -- the whole session hangs with it.

Simply breaking out of the loop is not enough: the code right after it calls
abort() for any non-zero ret, which would only convert the hang into a fatal
crash of the same critical process.  So bound the loop with a wall-clock
deadline (SVGA_EXECBUF_TIMEOUT_MS, default 2000 ms) and, on timeout, return the
errno to the caller instead of aborting.  vmw_swc_flush() turns that into
PIPE_ERROR_OUT_OF_MEMORY after completing its normal unwind, so the frame is
dropped and rendering continues.  Genuine errors still abort() exactly as
before.

Setting SVGA_EXECBUF_TIMEOUT_MS to a small value is a convenient way to
exercise the give-up path.

